WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Elsa Hosk, welcome!

00:00:03.143 --> 00:00:05.143
Thanks so much, Fredrik!

00:00:08.372 --> 00:00:10.395
Wonderful to be here!

00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:14.551
Tell me, Elsa, when you walk a catwalk,

00:00:14.551 --> 00:00:20.250
and you've been preparing for weeks and months for this,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.088
what is happening in your head as you walk?

00:00:26.643 --> 00:00:28.643
Ah, they're different!

00:00:28.941 --> 00:00:34.685
But I think that when I walk for for example Victoria's Secret, where the audience is a bit bigger,

00:00:34.861 --> 00:00:38.072
you probably think more about

00:00:38.918 --> 00:00:40.918
not falling over.

00:00:40.918 --> 00:00:42.671
That you can't mess up! 

00:00:42.905 --> 00:00:46.407
But other than that I feel like I black out.

00:00:47.378 --> 00:00:52.539
It just becomes completely black, and I just do my thing, and then it's suddenly over. It goes so fast.

00:00:52.674 --> 00:00:54.674
But have you ever messed up?

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.628
I did one casting once, for John Galliano,

00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:03.973
when I was really young, I was 14.

00:01:05.169 --> 00:01:09.994
And it was one of my first big castings in Paris.

00:01:10.427 --> 00:01:16.418
And I remember entering a big room, and there were lots of girls in there, 

00:01:16.419 --> 00:01:21.513
and it was very crowded, and I was really young and didn't quite understand how this worked.

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:26.076
And someone pushed me in front of John Galliano,

00:01:26.076 --> 00:01:29.511
and I fell flat right in front of him.

00:01:29.647 --> 00:01:35.032
And then ... I got the job anyways, and I'm not quite sure how that happened! 

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:37.440
Maybe he felt sorry for me!

00:01:37.441 --> 00:01:43.073
When you do this kind of work, Elsa,

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:48.600
do you play a part, or do you bring yourself out there?

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:55.369
Sometimes when you do fashion shows, you get words that they write down, that can be for example «aggressive»,

00:01:55.571 --> 00:01:58.019
«confident», or it can be like

00:01:59.036 --> 00:02:00.251
«pissed off».

00:02:00.252 --> 00:02:02.252
They'll give you a little directive

00:02:02.618 --> 00:02:06.000
about how to look when you walk the runway.

00:02:06.136 --> 00:02:12.373
But often, I think you just need to tell yourself to just think about what you're wearing,

00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:16.197
what kind of person would wear that outfit,

00:02:16.198 --> 00:02:19.588
what kind of person you should play in front of the cameras.

00:02:20.156 --> 00:02:23.546
Does it ever happen that you get as simple directions, Sofia?

00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:26.211
That can absolutely happen!

00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:31.238
Directions can be in a lot of different ways, and sometimes you'll get straight forward directives like that.

00:02:31.406 --> 00:02:33.563
May I ask a question? What's it like ...

00:02:33.929 --> 00:02:36.938
If you're supposed to seem incredible confident and ...

00:02:37.147 --> 00:02:41.411
Acting is partly about showing the cracks

00:02:41.674 --> 00:02:44.010
in this person, to show that to the audience.

00:02:44.376 --> 00:02:51.846
But what's that like when you get home, and aren't allowed to show a single crack when you've been working an entire day, what's that like afterwards?

00:02:53.173 --> 00:03:00.269
That's tough. I think that's been the toughest thing about the model industry, that you

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.670
often show a side of you that's not real.

00:03:04.914 --> 00:03:07.990
So when you get home, you feel quite ...

00:03:08.984 --> 00:03:12.000
Like «no one touch me, people have been touching me all day!».

00:03:12.001 --> 00:03:15.952
You've been playing this really positive, happy, confident person.

00:03:16.294 --> 00:03:18.944
So then it can feel like you want to ...

00:03:19.984 --> 00:03:23.150
I do think that the industry as it is

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:26.255
is leaning towards an ideal that's more real,

00:03:26.384 --> 00:03:29.303
that allows you to show that you have a story,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:37.499
and that the models who are interesting right now are those who have a background and those who have had

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:43.715
a struggle or something that makes them unique and that makes them inspiring instead of just having

00:03:43.902 --> 00:03:45.723
a pretty face.

00:03:45.723 --> 00:03:50.503
Tell me a little bit more about that, about how this ideal

00:03:50.503 --> 00:03:54.454
has changed since you began, because you've been doing this for a while.

00:03:55.992 --> 00:04:00.413
So much! I remember that when I started as a model ...

00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:04.865
If you had something like me, who had an alcohol abuse problem for example,

00:04:04.917 --> 00:04:07.186
it was nothing you wanted to talk about at all.

00:04:07.395 --> 00:04:14.910
But the clients could get scared. «What if she'll turn up for work drunk?».

00:04:14.911 --> 00:04:20.588
People didn't have that acceptance towards models. And now I think that it's more like 

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.279
you get encouraged to have a story.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.343
And that that's what gives you an inspiring face.

00:04:28.344 --> 00:04:34.088
That they'll put you on the cover of a publication and people will buy it because this girl has a 

00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:37.001
story that's inspiring to a lot of people.

00:04:37.001 --> 00:04:39.494
You became a model very early?

00:04:40.421 --> 00:04:41.435
How old were you?

00:04:41.436 --> 00:04:45.768
Yes, absolutely. I started working as a model when I was 13. 

00:04:47.635 --> 00:04:49.657
So it was early. But it was

00:04:49.657 --> 00:04:53.391
my dad who sent in photos to an agency with the thoughts that

00:04:53.391 --> 00:04:57.857
«Maybe she'll get the occasional job and make a little extra money.»

00:04:57.858 --> 00:05:02.347
No one ever thought that would turn out to be anything special, I think.

00:05:02.348 --> 00:05:03.900
Especially not me!

00:05:04.064 --> 00:05:07.208
We have one of those photos here.

00:05:07.208 --> 00:05:09.724
Who were you at this point in time, Elsa? 

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.550
Who was I then? I was really ... 

00:05:12.829 --> 00:05:16.085
Oh my God! My dad took that!

00:05:16.465 --> 00:05:20.348
What I remember from ...

00:05:20.894 --> 00:05:24.306
I feel like I've got a bad memory!

00:05:24.695 --> 00:05:26.695
But what I remember ... I think I was quite the tomboy.

00:05:26.696 --> 00:05:28.696
I spent a lot of time on my own.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:32.004
I wasn't the most popular girl in school, absolutely not.

00:05:32.004 --> 00:05:34.542
I kept to myself.

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:40.541
I was maybe a little uncomfortable in my own person.

00:05:41.163 --> 00:05:43.253
And I think that's what led me to

00:05:43.349 --> 00:05:46.896
wanting to fly away. The modeling was,

00:05:46.897 --> 00:05:48.897
when I could start doing that early, it felt like

00:05:49.375 --> 00:05:53.841
a fantastic world to be able to enter.

00:05:54.297 --> 00:05:56.588
And that become a thing.

00:05:56.842 --> 00:06:02.586
But did you travel with your parents to these jobs, or

00:06:02.586 --> 00:06:03.689
how did you do it?

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.372
My mom came with me quite often when I traveled.

00:06:07.576 --> 00:06:13.522
I began getting work when I was around 14, in Milan and Paris, and we went to Tokyo.

00:06:14.105 --> 00:06:17.607
And we'd stay there a month. And my mom always went with me until I was 18.

00:06:17.607 --> 00:06:21.625
And it was absolutely not what I wanted then.

00:06:21.626 --> 00:06:24.209
But when I think about it now, it was a good thing

00:06:24.508 --> 00:06:27.719
that she came with. Who knows what would've happened if not!

00:06:27.905 --> 00:06:32.708
But when you then got back home and were supposed to live a teenager's life,

00:06:32.709 --> 00:06:35.718
how were you affected by having started so early?

00:06:36.142 --> 00:06:38.747
That was the thing that was a little difficult.

00:06:38.747 --> 00:06:44.312
When you start working that early ... A lot of people who I've spoken to who started working early feel that

00:06:44.409 --> 00:06:48.405
you don't really fit in when you get back to school.

00:06:48.405 --> 00:06:51.391
You've developed so much more.

00:06:51.391 --> 00:07:00.233
I would hang out with people who were 30-40, it was very special to be able to work and be a professional at that young age.

00:07:00.483 --> 00:07:03.313
So when I then got back to school, I was like ...

00:07:03.971 --> 00:07:05.971
It was two completely different worlds.

00:07:05.972 --> 00:07:11.470
And then you ended up in something you describe as a type of abuse.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:17.887
The abuse started later, I think I was ... I started drinking quite late.

00:07:17.888 --> 00:07:23.789
It went quite quickly downhill, which I think is quite a usual story when you become addicted.

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:27.045
Especially for women, that it goes quite quickly when you ...

00:07:27.157 --> 00:07:29.157
You run yourself down quite quickly.

00:07:29.433 --> 00:07:32.733
Was it just alcohol or did you do drugs as well?

00:07:32.734 --> 00:07:34.218
It was everything.

00:07:34.218 --> 00:07:39.873
It was really everything that could help me escape myself, I think.

00:07:41.079 --> 00:07:45.500
That felt good to me, to be able to escape from 

00:07:45.585 --> 00:07:46.957
the person that I was.

00:07:47.009 --> 00:07:50.937
And the life that I had. Even though it wasn't a bad life in any way, it was just

00:07:51.084 --> 00:07:54.000
this feeling of being able to disappear. 

00:07:54.279 --> 00:07:55.808
How bad would it get?

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:00.000
I ended up at the bottom when I was ... So, I moved to New York

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.193
when I was 20.

00:08:01.671 --> 00:08:04.904
By then I had been drinking for maybe two years.

00:08:05.443 --> 00:08:10.380
And it went downhill quickly as I mentioned, and then when I got to New York,

00:08:10.769 --> 00:08:14.114
I went to a job in Los Angeles,

00:08:14.371 --> 00:08:16.797
and I had an overdose.

00:08:17.093 --> 00:08:19.093
And I ended up in the hospital.

00:08:19.968 --> 00:08:23.336
And then I went to rehab.

00:08:23.707 --> 00:08:26.402
But you're so incredibly

00:08:26.679 --> 00:08:28.881
manipulative when you're in that situation.

00:08:28.882 --> 00:08:34.918
Because you don't really know yourself what ... That's why I think it's great talking about this a lot now.

00:08:35.441 --> 00:08:41.656
You need to hear, when you're an alcoholic or stuck in an abuse, that there is

00:08:41.791 --> 00:08:43.321
another way.

00:08:43.498 --> 00:08:48.906
You don't have to be stuck in an abuse. 

00:08:48.906 --> 00:08:50.906
It's possible to escape.

00:08:51.093 --> 00:08:53.093
What did you overdose on?

00:08:53.257 --> 00:08:55.257
It was cocaine, I think.

00:08:55.444 --> 00:08:58.184
I remember waking up in a hospital.

00:08:59.787 --> 00:09:04.029
I remember that I was working at a beach, and then I woke up in a hospital

00:09:04.496 --> 00:09:07.236
with lots of tubes and stuff.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:13.055
And then I went back to New York. The memories are very fuzzy.

00:09:14.091 --> 00:09:19.073
And what's crazy is that when I think back on it, I remember thinking then that

00:09:19.304 --> 00:09:22.089
I wasn't the one with a problem, everyone else had a problem.

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:25.367
That everyone around me didn't understand.

00:09:25.621 --> 00:09:28.350
That's how I felt. 

00:09:28.507 --> 00:09:33.085
And I think that that's quite normal, that it takes a while before you understand

00:09:34.102 --> 00:09:37.088
that you're abusing and that it's not normal

00:09:37.218 --> 00:09:42.716
to go through life with drugs and alcohol every day.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.320
Sofia, you had a question?

00:09:44.321 --> 00:09:45.581
Yes, I wonder ...

00:09:45.582 --> 00:09:52.828
Because most people aren't able to stop abusing, it's about the hardest thing you can do.

00:09:52.949 --> 00:09:56.877
What is the reason you're able to stay sober today?

00:09:56.878 --> 00:10:02.286
Absolutely, it's so normal, I think that you're quite lucky if you're able to get out of abuse, 

00:10:02.286 --> 00:10:06.954
any kind of abuse. I really do think it's the same thing for people who are

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.038
suffering from gambling addiction or sex addiction or alcohol,

00:10:11.203 --> 00:10:13.673
you want to escape from yourself, quite simply.

00:10:14.368 --> 00:10:20.986
But I was incredibly lucky when I got back to New York after my rehab.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:26.514
I had an agent who was part of a program that exists all over the world.

00:10:26.835 --> 00:10:30.472
And she had saved a number on my phone 

00:10:30.933 --> 00:10:36.565
and she said to me that «whenever you need it» ... She had understood that I was like her, she was part of this program.

00:10:36.976 --> 00:10:43.505
And she told me that I had to do this on my own terms, no one can tell you

00:10:43.983 --> 00:10:50.915
«you have to get sober». You don't understand that, you have to reach the bottom and go from there.

00:10:51.319 --> 00:10:52.445
So she ...

00:10:52.766 --> 00:10:59.160
When I ended up at the bottom, I called this number, and there was a girl who was a part of this program and who took me to all these meetings

00:10:59.863 --> 00:11:04.396
and we did all the work you need to do when you're in this program.

00:11:04.767 --> 00:11:08.605
And I went every day, and I still go.

00:11:09.687 --> 00:11:15.072
That helped me. And it took a long while before I understood that

00:11:16.035 --> 00:11:19.896
«I'm like all the other people who are sitting in this room».

00:11:19.896 --> 00:11:26.425
I thought that «I'll trick them a little. I'll go to these meetings and then I'll get my old life back». But then 

00:11:26.585 --> 00:11:28.585
slowly but surely, I began to

00:11:29.355 --> 00:11:36.960
realize that «Oh, wow. I'm exactly like these people in here and I have to do exactly what they're doing to stay sober».

00:11:37.339 --> 00:11:42.971
So it's really thanks to that program that I have a career now.

00:11:43.365 --> 00:11:49.154
And your career really took off after this happened, right?

00:11:49.699 --> 00:11:53.291
Absolutely. It was definitely after, and it took a while.

00:11:53.705 --> 00:11:57.543
And then I was forced to really get to know myself again and really

00:11:57.543 --> 00:12:00.843
be okay with the person that I was sober

00:12:00.843 --> 00:12:04.278
without everything that had made me comfortable earlier.

00:12:04.922 --> 00:12:11.249
So that was really interesting, to learn to get to know myself. And then

00:12:11.566 --> 00:12:14.171
begin to show up to work and

00:12:14.268 --> 00:12:16.851
go to castings and to a good job,

00:12:16.852 --> 00:12:21.901
and be kind to everyone and be confident and everything you need to be as a model.

00:12:22.084 --> 00:12:25.407
I think that ... I sometimes work with child actors and things like that,

00:12:25.534 --> 00:12:30.292
and I've thought a lot about how you're affected by getting that kind of attention

00:12:30.763 --> 00:12:34.220
at an age when you're that easily shaped.

00:12:34.614 --> 00:12:36.973
I think it's interrelated that you ...

00:12:37.182 --> 00:12:39.989
I mean, a 14-year-old's brain isn't fully developed.

00:12:40.153 --> 00:12:43.700
Is it interrelated that it ended like that, or?

00:12:43.819 --> 00:12:44.586
What do you think?

00:12:44.952 --> 00:12:47.580
The best thing is to be a child as long as possible.

00:12:47.896 --> 00:12:50.434
I think that now, as I'm going to be a mother, I feel that ...

00:12:50.621 --> 00:12:56.477
I know the feeling of being 13 and being like «No! I want to go to Milan and I don't want to go to school» and blah, blah, blah. 

00:12:56.478 --> 00:12:58.007
It was really me who pushed for it.

00:12:58.225 --> 00:13:02.019
And it's hard as a parent to put the breaks on, I can imagine.

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:05.858
But I think it's best to try to stay a child for as long as possible.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.942
Elsa, you mention as a side-note

00:13:09.942 --> 00:13:16.179
that you're becoming a mother. I know that you've become used to the thought, but

00:13:16.179 --> 00:13:18.807
this was news this week.

00:13:18.807 --> 00:13:21.614
You published it on Instagram.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.000
We have the image here.

00:13:31.196 --> 00:13:34.205
How much was this planned?

00:13:34.419 --> 00:13:35.814
It was very much planned!

00:13:36.071 --> 00:13:39.192
We were so lucky. I tricked my guy a little because

00:13:39.192 --> 00:13:43.498
I thought that it would happen fast for us, for some reason that was in my head.

00:13:44.106 --> 00:13:47.070
And he wasn't quite ready. So I said

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:51.491
«It'll take a really long time, it'll be a year before it happens!»

00:13:51.791 --> 00:13:53.791
So he was just like «Okay, let's go»!

00:13:54.001 --> 00:13:56.786
And then we were just lucky enough to have it happen at once.

00:13:57.055 --> 00:13:58.674
It was planned.

00:13:59.399 --> 00:14:02.924
Do you feel ready? You're in the fifth month.

00:14:02.925 --> 00:14:06.808
I feel ready. I feel

00:14:07.263 --> 00:14:12.000
like I'm on a rollercoaster. Sometimes I panic. Sometimes I feel ready. 

00:14:12.178 --> 00:14:17.653
But for the most part I feel like we need something ...

00:14:18.001 --> 00:14:21.638
We're so selfish, we need something that's ...

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:27.188
We need to focus on something other than ourselves. I feel ready for that kind of responsibility.

00:14:27.387 --> 00:14:30.306
You can always ask Fredrik, he has so many children! 

00:14:30.514 --> 00:14:31.617
He can be a mentor. 

00:14:31.617 --> 00:14:32.541
Yes, exactly!

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:34.682
Do you have any advice for me?

00:14:35.677 --> 00:14:38.708
Yes, I have a help line that you can call!

00:14:38.799 --> 00:14:43.153
Where I answer questions from people who have them!

00:14:45.192 --> 00:14:50.600
I've heard that there's a program where you can call people who are mothers.

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:53.154
It's great! You should really be one.

00:14:53.155 --> 00:14:54.146
Why not a father?!

00:14:54.147 --> 00:14:56.147
Yes. Yes! 

00:14:56.147 --> 00:15:00.252
Exactly! I know that you're moving to L.A. and that your apartment is out for sale.

00:15:00.947 --> 00:15:02.947
I wonder ...

00:15:03.092 --> 00:15:10.922
How much do you miss walking the catwalk for lingerie brand Victoria's Secret?

00:15:10.922 --> 00:15:12.922
They've stopped doing their fashion shows.

00:15:15.413 --> 00:15:17.704
I think that I ...

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:24.000
When it happened every year, the show itself was always on my birthday.

00:15:24.744 --> 00:15:29.076
So the most pressure I felt during the entire year

00:15:29.076 --> 00:15:31.233
was on my birthday every year.

00:15:31.375 --> 00:15:38.644
That's been wonderful, to have a birthday where you can relax a little instead of 

00:15:38.644 --> 00:15:42.819
doing a show and having all those eyes on you. But

00:15:42.819 --> 00:15:45.267
I don't think I miss it too much, if I'm being honest. 

00:15:45.491 --> 00:15:47.827
Elsa, thank you so much for being here tonight! 

00:15:48.275 --> 00:15:49.042
Thank you!

